instructTargetT -- SEQ 442 R1 (2026-10-28): an assign whose target is an instruct.

This file is not run alone. pop.sh builds one input per row: everything above this file's `stop();`, then the row's
statements, then a sentinel. One process per row, so a row that writes into a truth sentinel cannot reach the next.
Old-road rows (OR-) drop the two kantGrammar lines (its include and KantGrammar in the search line), so they run on
Grokking alone.

kantGrammar's EXPRESSIOn splits the = tier as built: everything left of = is the target (Tony, SEQ 442 R1). So in
`qa > qb || righty = 42` the target is the instruct `qa > qb || righty`, not righty. testOP runs it, it hands back the
shared true node (righty non-zero; the prelude sets 7, tester's righty is 13) or the shared FALSE node (righty 0, IT-4),
and opAssign writes 42 INTO THAT SENTINEL -- a later `qc > qa` (or `qa > qc`) then reads 42. In the three-part line both sides
hand back true, and setContent refuses the self-assign ("tried to set group to itself true").
Born red at SEQ 442 R1: IT-2's true and IT-4's false read 42, IT-2 and IT-3 carry no refusal, IT-3 carries the setContent line.
Intended: an instruct target is refused by name at build ("the left of = is an instruct, not a field", seal 151's
family), nothing is written, each sentinel reads what IT-0 reads (the uncorrupted control). IT-1 is the control: its target is plain righty, and it must keep
righty = 1 with no refusal (kant's || yields a truth, so 42 || ... leaves 1).
Tony's original line read `ga > qb`; ga (undeclared) and qa measured the same, so the rows use qa.

